Hi Emma,

I suspect you mean standardized estimates of factor loadings.

In general, there is no a strict inviolabe value, it may vary from field to filed and according to you theoretical assumptions and hypotheses.

The most frequent rule of tumb that I've met is that its absolute value should not fall down beyond 0.4, as in EFA, although I saw .2, .3, .7 as well.

There

Whether to drop respectime item or not depends on you and your problem. If you only do a measurement model, and you have enough itmes left you may want to drop it. If it is a secondary loading, and 'primary' loading from another factor is strong enough I'd leave it.

Best,
Oleksii Shestakovskyi,
independent researcher, Ukraine

